<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<worksheet xml:id="Math234Fa24-Midterm2" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
	<title>Math234Fa24-Midterm2</title>
	<exercise>
		<introduction>
			<p>
				Let <m>f(x,y) = 4xy+\cos(\pi x) + y^3.</m>
			</p>
		</introduction>
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					Find the tangent plane to <m>f</m> at <m>(2,-1).</m>
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					<me>
						\begin{aligned}
						f_x \amp = 4y - \pi \sin(\pi x) \implies f_x(2,-1) = -4 - \pi \sin(2\pi) = -4 \\
						f_y \amp = 4x + 3y^2 \implies f_y(2,-1) = 8 + 3 = 11 \\
						f(2,-1) \amp = 4(2)(-1) + \cos(2\pi) + (-1)^3 = -8 + 1 - 1 = -8
						\end{aligned}
					</me>
					The equation of the tangent plane is:
					<me>z = -4(x-2) + 11(y+1) - 8.</me>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</task>
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					Use the tangent plane to <m>f</m> at <m>(2,-1)</m> to approximate the value of <m>f(2.1, -0.9).</m>
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					<me>
						\begin{aligned}
						f(2.1, -0.9) \amp \approx -4(2.1-2) + 11(-0.9+1) - 8 \\
						\amp = -4(0.1) + 11(0.1) - 8 \\
						\amp = -0.4 + 1.1 - 8 = -7.3.
						\end{aligned}
					</me>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</task>
	</exercise>
	<exercise>
		<introduction>
			<p>
				Suppose <m>f(u,v)</m> is differentiable and <m>u=x-y</m> and <m>v=y-x</m>. Find <me>\dfrac{\partial
							f}{\partial x} +\dfrac{\partial f}{\partial y}.</me>
			</p>
		</introduction>
		<solution>
			<p>
				Using the chain rule:
				<me>
					\begin{aligned}
					\frac{\partial f}{\partial x} \amp = \frac{\partial f}{\partial u} \frac{\partial u}{\partial x} + \frac{\partial f}{\partial v} \frac{\partial v}{\partial x} = f_u(1) + f_v(-1) = f_u - f_v \\
					\frac{\partial f}{\partial y} \amp = \frac{\partial f}{\partial u} \frac{\partial u}{\partial y} + \frac{\partial f}{\partial v} \frac{\partial v}{\partial y} = f_u(-1) + f_v(1) = -f_u + f_v
					\end{aligned}
				</me>
				Adding them together:
				<me>\frac{\partial f}{\partial x} + \frac{\partial f}{\partial y} = (f_u - f_v) + (-f_u + f_v) = 0.</me>
			</p>
		</solution>
	</exercise>
	<exercise>
		<introduction>
			<p>
				Let <m>f(x,y) = e^{x^2 - y} + 2\sqrt{y}</m>.
			</p>
		</introduction>
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					Find the directional derivative of the function <m>f(x,y)</m> at the point <m>(2,4)</m> in the direction of the point <m>(3,-1).</m> That is, find the directional derivative at <m>(2,4)</m> as a particle is moving from <m>(2,4)</m> to <m>(3,-1).</m>
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					The vector from <m>(2,4)</m> to <m>(3,-1)</m> is <m>\vec{v} = \langle 3-2, -1-4 \rangle = \langle 1, -5 \rangle</m>.
					The distance is <m>|\vec{v}| = \sqrt{1 + 25} = \sqrt{26}</m>.
					The unit direction vector is <m>\vec{u} = \langle \frac{1}{\sqrt{26}}, -\frac{5}{\sqrt{26}} \rangle</m>.
					<me>
						\begin{aligned}
						\nabla f \amp = \langle 2x e^{x^2-y}, -e^{x^2-y} + y^{-1/2} \rangle \\
						\nabla f(2,4) \amp = \langle 4 e^0, -e^0 + 4^{-1/2} \rangle = \langle 4, -1 + \frac{1}{2} \rangle = \langle 4, -\frac{1}{2} \rangle \\
						D_{\vec{u}} f(2,4) \amp = \nabla f(2,4) \cdot \vec{u} = \langle 4, -\frac{1}{2} \rangle \cdot \langle \frac{1}{\sqrt{26}}, -\frac{5}{\sqrt{26}} \rangle = \frac{4 + \frac{5}{2}}{\sqrt{26}} = \frac{13}{2\sqrt{26}}
						\end{aligned}
					</me>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</task>
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					Based on your answer from (a), is the function <m>f(x,y)</m> increasing (that is, is the value of the function getting larger), or decreasing (that is, is the value of the function getting smaller) in that direction at the point <m>(2,4)</m>?
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					Increasing, since the directional derivative is positive.
				</p>
			</solution>
		</task>
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					In what direction does <m>f</m> have the maximum rate of change at the point <m>(2,4)</m>? Write your answer as a <term>unit</term> vector.
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					The direction of maximum increase is <m>\nabla f(2,4) = \langle 4, -\frac{1}{2} \rangle</m>.
					The unit vector is:
					<me>\vec{v}_{max} = \frac{\nabla f}{|\nabla f|} = \langle \frac{4}{\sqrt{16.25}}, -\frac{0.5}{\sqrt{16.25}} \rangle = \langle \frac{8}{\sqrt{65}}, -\frac{1}{\sqrt{65}} \rangle.</me>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</task>
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					What is the maximum rate of change of <m>f</m> at <m>(2,4)</m>?
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					The maximum rate of change is <m>|\nabla f(2,4)| = \sqrt{16 + 1/4} = \sqrt{65/4} = \frac{\sqrt{65}}{2}</m>.
				</p>
			</solution>
		</task>
	</exercise>
	<exercise>
		<introduction>
			<p>
				Consider the function <me>f(x,y) = 3x^2y + y^3-3y.</me>
			</p>
		</introduction>
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					Find all critical point(s) of <m>f(x,y)</m>.
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					<me>
						\begin{aligned}
						f_x \amp = 6xy = 0 \implies x=0 \text{ or } y=0 \\
						f_y \amp = 3x^2 + 3y^2 - 3 = 0 \implies x^2 + y^2 = 1
						\end{aligned}
					</me>
					If <m>x=0</m>, then <m>y^2 = 1 \implies y = \pm 1</m>. Points: <m>(0, 1), (0, -1)</m>.
					If <m>y=0</m>, then <m>x^2 = 1 \implies x = \pm 1</m>. Points: <m>(1, 0), (-1, 0)</m>.
					Critical points: <m>(0,1), (0,-1), (1,0), (-1,0)</m>.
				</p>
			</solution>
		</task>
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					Classify each critical point you found in (a) as a local maximum, local minimum, or saddle point.
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					<me>f_{xx} = 6y, \quad f_{xy} = 6x, \quad f_{yy} = 6y</me>
					<me>D = f_{xx}f_{yy} - (f_{xy})^2 = (6y)(6y) - (6x)^2 = 36y^2 - 36x^2</me>
					<ul>
						<li>At <m>(0, 1)</m>: <m>D = 36(1)^2 - 0 = 36 \gt 0</m>. Since <m>f_{xx} = 6 \gt 0 \implies</m> local minimum.</li>
						<li>At <m>(0, -1)</m>: <m>D = 36(-1)^2 - 0 = 36 \gt 0</m>. Since <m>f_{xx} = -6 \lt 0 \implies</m> local maximum.</li>
						<li>At <m>(1, 0)</m>: <m>D = 0 - 36 = -36 \lt 0 \implies</m> saddle point.</li>
						<li>At <m>(-1, 0)</m>: <m>D = 0 - 36(1) = -36 \lt 0 \implies</m> saddle point.</li>
					</ul>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</task>
	</exercise>
	<exercise>
		<introduction>
			<p>
				Find the absolute maximum and minimum values of the function <m>f(x,y)= xy</m> subject to the constraint <m>x^2+4y^2=2</m>.
			</p>
		</introduction>
		<solution>
			<p>
				Let <m>g(x,y) = x^2 + 4y^2 = 2</m>.
				<me>
					\begin{aligned}
					\nabla f \amp = \lambda \nabla g \\
					\langle y, x \rangle \amp = \lambda \langle 2x, 8y \rangle
					\end{aligned}
				</me>
				This gives:
				<ol>
					<li><m>y = 2\lambda x \implies \lambda = \frac{y}{2x}</m> (if <m>x \neq 0</m>)</li>
					<li><m>x = 8\lambda y \implies \lambda = \frac{x}{8y}</m> (if <m>y \neq 0</m>)</li>
				</ol>
				Equating <m>\lambda</m>: <m>\frac{y}{2x} = \frac{x}{8y} \implies 8y^2 = 2x^2 \implies x^2 = 4y^2</m>.
				Substitute into constraint: <m>4y^2 + 4y^2 = 2 \implies 8y^2 = 2 \implies y^2 = 1/4 \implies y = \pm 1/2</m>.
				Then <m>x^2 = 4(1/4) = 1 \implies x = \pm 1</m>.
				Compare values:
				<me>
					\begin{aligned}
					f(1, 1/2) \amp = 1/2 \\
					f(1, -1/2) \amp = -1/2 \\
					f(-1, 1/2) \amp = -1/2 \\
					f(-1, -1/2) \amp = 1/2
					\end{aligned}
				</me>
				The absolute maximum is <m>1/2</m>, and the absolute minimum is <m>-1/2</m>.
			</p>
		</solution>
	</exercise>
	<exercise>
		<introduction>
			<p>
				Compute the following integrals.
			</p>
		</introduction>
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					<m>\displaystyle\int_0^1 \int_{0}^{y} xe^{y^3}\, dx \, dy</m>
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					<me>
						\begin{aligned}
						\int_0^1 \int_0^y x e^{y^3} \, dx \, dy \amp = \int_0^1 \left[ \frac{x^2}{2} e^{y^3} \right]_0^y \, dy \\
						\amp = \int_0^1 \frac{y^2}{2} e^{y^3} \, dy \\
						\amp = \left[ \frac{1}{6} e^{y^3} \right]_0^1 = \frac{1}{6}(e - 1)
						\end{aligned}
					</me>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</task>
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					<m>\displaystyle\int_0^1 \int_y^{\sqrt{2-y^2}} \sqrt{x^2+y^2} \, dx \, dy</m>
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
                <p>
                    The region is a sector of a disk of radius <m>\sqrt{2}</m> from <m>\theta = 0</m> to <m>\theta = \pi/4</m>.
					<me>
						\begin{aligned}
						\int_0^{\pi/4} \int_0^{\sqrt{2}} r \cdot r \, dr \, d\theta \amp = \int_0^{\pi/4} \left[ \frac{r^3}{3} \right]_0^{\sqrt{2}} \, d\theta \\
						\amp = \int_0^{\pi/4} \frac{2\sqrt{2}}{3} \, d\theta = \frac{\pi\sqrt{2}}{6}
						\end{aligned}
					</me>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</task>
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					<m>\displaystyle\int_0^{\pi/2}\int_{x}^{\pi/2} \dfrac{\sin(y)}{y}\, dy\, dx</m>
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					Change order of integration: <m>0 \le x \le \pi/2, x \le y \le \pi/2</m> becomes <m>0 \le y \le \pi/2, 0 \le x \le y</m>.
					<me>
						\begin{aligned}
						\int_0^{\pi/2} \int_0^y \frac{\sin y}{y} \, dx \, dy \amp = \int_0^{\pi/2} y \cdot \frac{\sin y}{y} \, dy \\
						\amp = \int_0^{\pi/2} \sin y \, dy = [-\cos y]_0^{\pi/2} = 1
						\end{aligned}
					</me>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</task>
	</exercise>
	<exercise>
		<introduction>
			<p>
				Write down triple integrals that represent the volume of the following solids. You do <term>NOT</term> need to evaluate the integrals. Please note that this question has three parts, (a) and (b) on this page, and (c) on the next page.
			</p>
		</introduction>
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					The solid in the first octant that is bounded by the coordinate planes and the plane <m>x+y+z=3.</m>
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					<me>V = \int_0^3 \int_0^{3-x} \int_0^{3-x-y} 1 \, dz \, dy \, dx.</me>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</task>
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					The solid that lies in the first octant, above the paraboloid <m>z = x^2+y^2,</m> and below the paraboloid <m>z = 8-x^2-y^2.</m>
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					The intersection is <m>x^2 + y^2 = 8 - x^2 - y^2 \implies 2r^2 = 8 \implies r = 2</m>.
					In the first octant, <m>0 \le \theta \le \pi/2</m>.
					<me>V = \int_0^{\pi/2} \int_0^2 \int_{r^2}^{8-r^2} r \, dz \, dr \, d\theta.</me>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</task>
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					The solid that lies below the cone <m>z = -\sqrt{x^2+y^2},</m> and between the spheres <m>x^2+y^2+z^2=9</m> and <m>x^2+y^2+z^2=16.</m>
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					Using spherical coordinates: <m>3 \le \rho \le 4</m>, <m>0 \le \theta \le 2\pi</m>, and <m>3\pi/4 \le \phi \le \pi</m>.
					<me>V = \int_0^{2\pi} \int_{3\pi/4}^\pi \int_3^4 \rho^2 \sin \phi \, d\rho \, d\phi \, d\theta.</me>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</task>
	</exercise>
</worksheet>
